Thank you @pbronner and @ardent_vc for supporting us! 

@pbronner: "We backed @soothlabs at seed. Here's why. The team includes @subail, who founded and scaled Meta's Pittsburgh AI office and serves on CMU's Robotics Institute faculty. @rsalakhu Salakhutdinov is a professor at @SCSatCMU, a pioneer in modern generative modeling, Apple's first director of AI research, and a disciple of Geoffrey Hinton. Chuck Hoover directed billion-dollar innovation programs at Meta. This $50M round was led by @felicis at a $335M valuation, with angel investments from @ylecun and @JeffDean, and Andrew Bosworth advising. 

What they're building is a continuously trained world model for long-horizon forecasting. It takes verifiable propositions and returns calibrated probabilities with a structured causal timeline of driving events. Institutions commit capital in the face of deep uncertainty every day, and the stakes keep rising while the tools haven't moved. Strategy still gets set with gut calls, internal models, and the occasional expert opinion. Foresight should be a system, not a skill. LLMs aren't the answer here; they're optimized to generate plausible text, not to produce probabilities graded against real events. Sooth is built on a different architecture designed for exactly that job, with a causal chain users can interrogate, revise, and stress-test as conditions change. Congrats to Yaser, Russ, Chuck, and the team."

Sooth Labs is pursuing an ambitious goal: building a world model for global foresight. Most existing systems are reactive, narrow, or static, failing to account for second-order effects and distribution shifts, and they struggle to reason across multiple domains like geopolitics, economics, climate, and technology. This results in predictable events catching us by surprise, leading to misallocated capital, poorly designed policies, and fragile mental models.

If Sooth Labs can make progress in this area, it could significantly change decision-making processes at all levels, from governments to markets to large-scale AI systems.

@rsalakhu: "Foresight will be the defining frontier on the path to AGI. I am excited to start Sooth Labs with my amazing co-founders: Yaser Sheikh, Chuck Hoover, David LaRose, and Shih-En Wei. Deeply grateful to Aydin Senkut and Feyza Haskaraman at Felicis for leading the round, alongside an exceptional group of partners."

New work on scaling test-time compute for agentic coding introduces a framework that converts rollouts into structured summaries capturing key hypotheses, progress, and failure modes while discarding low-signal details. This enables two forms of inference-time scaling: Recursive Tournament Voting (RTV) for parallel selection through iterative small-group comparisons, and Parallel-Distill-Refine (PDR) for sequential improvement by conditioning new rollouts on distilled summaries.

The approach consistently boosts performance on frontier benchmarks, with Claude-4.5-Opus improving from 70.9% to 77.6% on SWE-Bench Verified, and from 46.9% to 59.1% on Terminal-Bench v2.0. These gains highlight that effective test-time scaling for long-horizon agents relies on representation, selection, and reuse, rather than just sampling more trajectories.

@anirudhg9119: "How do coding agents get better from experience? Past attempts as interface: Turn rollouts into reusable summaries that future attempts can build on."

How well do today’s frontier models handle long-horizon, multi-step web agent tasks, such as identifying the top 25 U.S. CS PhD programs with ML/AI faculty likely accepting students and compiling the results into a structured sheet? Check out our new work on Odysseys, a benchmark of 200 long-horizon tasks derived from real browsing sessions and evaluated on the live Internet.

We show that binary pass/fail is inadequate in this setting and propose rubric-based evaluation, which better aligns with human judgment and provides more informative signals. Across leading models, the best achieves only 44.5% success, leaving substantial headroom. We further introduce a Trajectory Efficiency metric (rubric score per step) and find efficiency remains extremely low at 1.15%, highlighting a key bottleneck. Odysseys provides a realistic benchmark for measuring progress toward web agents capable of sustained, efficient, real-world operation.

@kohjingyu: "One of the things I’m most excited about this year is building agents that can work productively for hours, days, or weeks. Coding agents are starting to become very competent at this, but what about computer use agents? Our new benchmark, Odysseys, is a set of 200 new tasks derived from real-world browsing behavior that measure long-horizon web navigation capabilities. Interestingly, we find that frontier CUAs are already surprisingly good at working productively for up to an hour on these tasks, but there’s a lot of work to be done in making them even more efficient."

How well do today’s frontier models handle long-horizon, multi-step web agent tasks, such as identifying in-person, 6-week, on-campus university engineering summer programs for high school students and compiling the results into a structured sheet? Check out our new work on Odysseys: Benchmarking Web Agents on Realistic Long Horizon Tasks.

Odysseys introduces a benchmark of 200 long-horizon tasks derived from real browsing sessions and evaluated on the live Internet. The study shows that binary pass/fail is inadequate, proposing rubric-based evaluation that aligns better with human judgment and provides more informative signals. Across leading models, the best achieves only 44.5% success, indicating substantial room for improvement. A new Trajectory Efficiency metric reveals extremely low efficiency at 1.15%, highlighting a key bottleneck. Odysseys provides a realistic benchmark for measuring progress toward web agents capable of sustained, efficient, real-world operation.

@JangLawrenceK: "AI agents can work pretty well on the web now for short tasks. I wanted to know: could they go longer, on harder tasks? Can an agent plan 2 weddings in different cities and a honeymoon within the same month, or find the most suitable culinary arts school across the US for my post-PhD plans? We are releasing Odysseys: a benchmark of 200 long-horizon web agent tasks evaluated on the live internet. All our tasks are inspired by real human data and many take hours to complete. The best frontier model we tested (Claude Opus 4.6) reaches only 44.5% perfect-task success, leaving substantial room for improvement. I donated a couple of my own automation wishes to this benchmark. My favorite contribution was to 'Rank the top 10 ACL + Meniscus surgeons in the area' - as this took me a decent amount of time to do myself when I got my own knee fixed. GPT 5.5 was able to do this for me with 4 dollars and 30 minutes!"

How successfully and efficiently can agents carry out long-horizon tasks on the web? We built a benchmark of approximately 200 multi-site tasks based on people's real browsing history, many of which take hours to solve.

Led by @JangLawrenceK and @kohjingyu, with @rsalakhu, the new benchmark Odysseys measures long-horizon web navigation capabilities, derived from real-world browsing behavior. Interestingly, frontier computer use agents are already surprisingly good at working productively for up to an hour on these tasks, but there’s still much work to be done to enhance their efficiency.

@kohjingyu: "One of the things I’m most excited about this year is building agents that can work productively for hours, days, or weeks. Coding agents are starting to become very competent at this, but what about computer use agents?" The linked benchmark evaluates 200 long-horizon web agent tasks with rubric-based scoring on the live internet.

